# v2 SFT data audit — 2026-11-17

**Verdict: NO-GO for training the published v2 revision.** There is confirmed train/dev screen leakage, demonstrably incompatible action supervision, and premature completion labels. Valid JSON and matching builder statistics do not clear this mix.

Audited dataset: `experimentalmachines/AndroidAgent-SFT-v2` at **`8e0e63a48f180aa60c103d2f492d6364355cb306`**. Downloaded exactly **three v2 train shards, 00000/00003/00004**, selected with seed `20261117`, and all three dev shards: **15,000 train + 1,999 dev = 16,999 rows**. Dataset files/card/stats totaled **1,626,522,302 bytes**. The remaining 13 train shards were not downloaded or inspected. Findings are confirmed instances or explicitly bounded sample results, not a certification of all 79,991 advertised train rows.

I decoded and hashed every downloaded image, ran the actual strict action parser and resize rule, joined primary-source annotations, inspected **40 random train rows per new source**, and performed 27 additional targeted review instances, some overlapping the random sample, plus five AC screens for app-identity review. Red crosshairs use `agent_format.norm_to_px`; images were not generated or altered except for analytical overlays. No GPU training or live device replay was performed.

| ID | Severity | Finding and evidence | Exact fix before training |
|---|---|---|---|
| F1 | **blocking** | **Five train/dev Odyssey pairs have identical JPEG bytes and decoded pixels**, despite different episode IDs. Two are PIN-entry screens and three are launchers; several also have closely related goals. All pairs are listed below. | Compute canonical screenshot hashes across **all** candidate train/dev rows before publishing. Remove train rows with dev hashes, or assign connected episode/screen groups to one split. Include reviewed near-duplicate clusters and task-template siblings in the split audit. Assert zero train/dev image-hash intersection on the rebuilt full dataset. |
| F2 | **major** | **The shared type-only contract differs from the AndroidWorld executor.** The actual executor function, run with recording ADB stubs, executes `type_text("Art"), Enter, Enter` for `input_text("Art")` followed by `keyboard_enter`. **99/100** inspected AMEX Enter rows immediately follow raw `TYPE` steps. v1 has no `keyboard_enter` targets; v2 introduces this explicit second-submit pattern. [Probe](data_v2_cache/executor_probe.json), [executor](../../android_world/android_world/env/actuation.py), [protocol](../../docs/EVAL_PROTOCOL.md). | Resolve the execution contract explicitly. To retain the shared format, implement a type-only agent adapter and compare **both v1 and v2** with that adapter; keep standard-protocol AW scores separately labeled. If standard AW auto-Enter must remain, retain only trajectories whose observations/actions remain valid under it. Do not blindly delete Enter rows or fabricate intermediate screenshots. This is a pre-existing v1 mismatch that the new Enter allocation makes more consequential. |
| F3 | **major** | **Cardinal direction does not establish executable scroll semantics.** AMEX `87dde1a8dfb3407a980f2fbc9e4572ff/12` scrolls a narrow time dropdown. Raw finger path is `(946,725)→(967,1547)` on 1440×3040. The actual AW `scroll up` path is `(720,1520)→(720,3040)`, outside the dropdown. [Overlay](data_v2_cache/visual/amex_21_scroll_paths.png). Builder accepts it at `data/build_v2_mix.py:309`. | Reject region-specific scrolls, drags, and other gestures that the fixed center executor cannot reproduce, and truncate dependent history at that step. Alternatively extend and test a shared region-aware action contract in builder, trainer and executor before including them. Checking only angle and distance is insufficient. |
| F4 | **major** | **AMEX key-like text is treated as literal typing.** Five downloaded train targets are `input_text("esc")`; `53cc23e9f1c0492c900cf960c4a4a9a7/13` is a non-editable article screen. Independent reconstruction selects **17 `esc` targets and one newline-only target** before image filtering. Two inspected histories also contain newline-only typing. [Article example](data_v2_cache/visual/extra_amex_05.png), [selection](data_v2_cache/amex_selected_before_images.jsonl). | Validate `TYPE` payload semantics. Quarantine Escape-like actions and their dependent suffixes unless source execution establishes a supported equivalent. Map genuine Enter events to `keyboard_enter` only after resolving F2; preserve actual multiline text. Do not train literal `esc`, or automatically assume Escape equals Back. |
| F5 | **major** | **Source terminal markers are not reliable goal-completion evidence.** Odyssey `5965127172744421/14` and `4704062497509653/11` say complete while Play Store still says **Pending**. `9545981158176326/8` maps `INCOMPLETE` to infeasible merely because a Facebook search did not find the desired information. [Terminal sheet](data_v2_cache/visual/terminals_sheet_1.jpg), [incomplete example](data_v2_cache/visual/odyssey_14.png), raw reasons in [provenance](data_v2_cache/provenance.json). | Require reviewed post-goal observations for complete labels; discard pending/loading endpoints when no later observation exists. Distinguish abandoned/unsuccessful attempts from genuine infeasibility. Quarantine unreviewed `INCOMPLETE`/`TASK_IMPOSSIBLE` labels rather than filling terminal quotas with them. |
| F6 | **major** | **Some information-seeking trajectories end silently.** AMEX `14eeb4cbf4674199bbb878225d0773f4/13` asks for the latest show time; the screenshot shows times, but supervision is `status complete`. Eight sampled train complete rows have question-mark goals and need review; this heuristic is not itself an error count. [Example](data_v2_cache/visual/amex_20.png). AW stores the answer text and ends on `answer` in `eval/androidworld/lfm_agent.py:178`. | Review terminal goals requiring a response. Emit a screenshot/history-supported `answer` where the answer is verified; otherwise drop that terminal. Keep factual answers distinct from task-status acknowledgments across every source, not just ScreenQA. |
| F7 | **major** | **Additional source-label contradictions survive the filters.** Odyssey `1252281995307488/0` points outside the stated recents card. AMEX `1cb9048b34114235b0c8031feb3612f0/10` selects `mark.tylor@gmail.com` instead of required `mark.taylor@gmail.com`; `aca93ad65ded4e1598871da20bc5b479/5` types “morning mediation” instead of “Morning Meditation.” ScreenQA `rico10893/10963` omits visible “Basic Concepts.” [Row-level judgments](data_v2_cache/visual_review.md). | Add goal/entity consistency and visual target checks. Correct labels only when the intended target/answer is supported by the observation. For trajectory errors, quarantine the erroneous step and dependent suffix; changing an earlier typed string without re-recording later screenshots would invent a trajectory. Correct/drop the static QA independently. |
| F8 | **major** | **The blanket AW-app exclusion claim is too broad, and the planned 14-app claim remains partly unverified.** Chrome is present by exact package in AMEX and ScreenQA; Settings/Clock/Contacts appear in AC, and Settings/Chrome/Clock are visible in Odyssey. For the intended 14 third-party app families, no exact package hit was found in inspected AMEX/ScreenQA rows, but AC lacks package provenance, five AC rows refer to `Calendar Pro`, and **106 sampled train + 60 dev Odyssey rows** have unresolved `Calendar` app labels. | State a frozen, explicit exclusion policy: the 14 held-out app families versus shared built-in apps. Retain a per-step package/app provenance manifest. Resolve Calendar/Calendar Pro and other ambiguous aliases to packages or conservatively exclude their episodes, as the earlier viability review required for Calendar. Re-audit AC with source package evidence. Do not describe the present dataset as excluding every AW app. |
| F9 | **minor** | **The advertised AMEX three-row episode cap is bypassed by quota selection.** Running the real sampler on a fixture returns six rows from one episode with cap three. Reconstructing all AMEX candidates yields **19 selected episodes above three, maximum seven** before image filtering. All 1,469 inspected AMEX train keys match that reconstruction; the three-shard sample alone has no observed cap violation. [Reproduction](data_v2_cache/sampler_repro.json), [full reconstruction](data_v2_cache/provenance.json). | Apply uniqueness, total-row limit and episode cap during **every quota pass**, then during filler selection. Retain the existing exclusion of quota categories from the filler. A corrected simulation still obtains 8,000 rows and all four quotas, with maximum three per episode. [Simulation](data_v2_cache/sampler_fixed_simulation.json). |
| F10 | **minor** | **AMEX reasoning is largely an action template, not an intent.** All **801/801 sampled AMEX train clicks** say “Tap the next element needed for the task.” This is 54.5% of sampled AMEX rows. The text adds no target/state information to an intent-in-`<think>` model or its eight-step history. Harm to model accuracy is a risk inference, not a measured training result. | **Blank templated nonterminal AMEX `reasoning_content` and corresponding history intent strings**, preserving action JSON and `history_text` formatting. Retain only validated terminal intents or genuinely reviewed, screenshot-grounded target intents. Do not generate speculative reasoning merely to fill the field. Verify empty-think rendering for the actual training template. |
| F11 | **minor** | **Duplicates exist, but most are not identical training examples.** Six excess train rows share image+action; only **one** also has identical messages: AC episodes `8110/0` and `14189/0`. ScreenQA `rico40184/38672` and `/38675` ask different questions with the same answer, “disabled.” | Deduplicate exact image+messages examples. Separately cap repeated screen/action weighting and inspect repeated no-change decisions. Do not discard valid different QA questions solely because image+answer matches. |
| F12 | **minor** | **Coordinate-frame provenance is incomplete.** AMEX `ba060ad1f8c04af1afad922de5f78906/9` declares a 3120×1440 device but has a 1440×3120 source image. Its normalized point still plausibly hits Favorite; this is **not a demonstrated missed click**. [Overlay](data_v2_cache/visual/amex_rotation.png). | Record the actual coordinate-frame transform and verify orientation mismatches against targets. Quarantine unresolved cases; do not blindly transpose dimensions, which would move this plausible point away from Favorite. Save raw action, screenshot ID/hash, dimensions and conversion rule per selected row. |

**Leakage evidence.** These are exact matches, not perceptual-hash guesses. All listed rows are Odyssey. Step numbers are the published `step_idx`.

| Train episode / step | Dev episode / step | Matching screen | Decoded-pixel SHA-256 prefix |
|---|---|---|---|
| `0156438551317768 / 5` | `2021810587842027 / 5` | PIN 147258 entry; digit 5 | `5caee15fce2748934` |
| `0623769107602047 / 0` | `3005377680340235 / 0` | Same launcher; Smart Cities lecture goals | `12025e8b0c461f3b9` |
| `8957119075221387 / 0` | `6716619738308638 / 6` | Same launcher; yoga/reminder goals | `cef016cb0c9879d47` |
| `9524026473502905 / 6` | `7291811791048259 / 6` | PIN 123654 entry; digit 4 | `054449d33709e0169` |
| `2709121280459435 / 0` | `9707452028352607 / 0` | Same launcher; TikTok/volume goals | `40cec38ffd866eb44` |

See [all hashes and row IDs](data_v2_cache/checks.json), [first four pairs](data_v2_cache/visual/leaks_sheet_1.jpg), and [fifth pair](data_v2_cache/visual/leaks_sheet_2.jpg). This is development-set contamination; it does **not** prove AndroidWorld benchmark-task leakage. Five dev images already intersect only 18.75% of the advertised train rows, so a full on-builder scan is required after rebuilding.

**Checks that passed, and their limits.**

| Check | Executed result |
|---|---|
| Train/dev episode overlap | **0** for source-qualified episode IDs across the 15,000 inspected train rows and all dev rows. This does not eliminate the image overlaps above. |
| AndroidControl official split | All **10,493** inspected AC rows are in freshly downloaded official **train** IDs: **0 test, 0 validation, 0 unknown**. Official lists contain 13,603 train / 137 validation / 1,543 test IDs. |
| Odyssey official split | All **2,941** inspected Odyssey rows match official random-split train/test membership, canonicalizing zero-padded IDs. |
| AMEX split and flags | All **1,969** inspected AMEX rows match the episode-hash holdout; none belongs to an episode flagged `need_human_check` or containing a nonfinal terminal marker. |
| ScreenQA split | Source train/validation image-ID intersection is **0** across the complete annotation files. All **1,596** downloaded QA rows join the correct question/image and a permitted source answer. No inspected ScreenQA train/dev pixel collision. |
| Messages and actions | All **16,999** rows have system/user/assistant order, the exact shared system prompt, string content, an assistant `reasoning_content` field, no low-level current-step hint, exactly one image, and assistant JSON equal to `gt_action`. **0** failures from `parse_action(strict=True)` or canonical action serialization. Thirteen AC intents are empty, inherited from v1; the contract requires the field, not fabricated text. |
| Images | Every image decodes and matches `smart_resize(orig_h, orig_w)` exactly. Sizes range across seven shapes, including **576×1312** and **640×1152**; all are portrait and within the pixel-area budget. The card's “880×880” should be described as an **area budget**, not a per-edge maximum. |
| History syntax | All rows round-trip through `agent_format.user_text/history_text`; numbering is contiguous and ends at the preceding source step, with at most eight entries. **All 1,596 ScreenQA histories are `None`.** |
| History causality | **5,469** cross-row historical links checked: AC 4,361, Odyssey 261, AMEX 847; **0 mismatches**. Entire supplied histories were additionally reconstructed from raw annotations for **1,969 AMEX rows and 291 Odyssey rows from 237 episode files**, with **0 mismatches**. Odyssey uses earlier `low_level_instruction`, not the post-hoc `context` field. This verifies chronology and construction, not the truth or executability of every source action/intent. F3/F4 show why that distinction matters. |
| Source action conversion | **1,120 AMEX points**, **356 AMEX scrolls**, and **32 Odyssey scrolls** joined raw labels/endpoints. No conversion mismatch in those checks. Extracted `swipe_dir` returns finger-up→down, finger-down→up, finger-left→right, finger-right→left; short/diagonal fixtures reject. Direction signs are correct; region and action semantics can still be wrong. |
| Terminal position | All **168 inspected AMEX terminal rows** are the last source step. All **174 inspected Odyssey terminals** also match the final source annotation. These are actual terminal-annotation screenshots, but F5 demonstrates that final source position does not establish completed user goals. AC terminals are copied from v1; original AC post-action screens were not newly reacquired. |
| v1 compatibility | Live v1 revision remains `1c49e7d117bd109eebd976b04deefa708b0e2007`. A fresh, range-read **3,020-row metadata sample** gives **312 overlapping AC high-mode rows** with identical messages/actions/dimensions/terminal flags after normalizing absent non-assistant reasoning to empty strings. All 10,493 v2 AC keys/actions also match the existing full v1 metadata snapshot at that same immutable revision. |
| Current trainer loader | **Pass after an external update during this audit.** The initial hardcoded nested path matched no v2 files. The current `_hub_data_files`/`load_any` were executed against live Hub inventories: v2 matches 16 train and one shard per dev split; v1 matches 32 train and two val shards. [Current probe](data_v2_cache/loader_current.json). The old mismatch is **resolved, not an outstanding blocker**. No full SFTTrainer/model batch was run. |

**AW app evidence.** The exact 14 families are the packages listed in the earlier viability review and the local AndroidWorld setup: Simple Calendar/Draw/Gallery/SMS, Markor, Pro Expense, Broccoli, Joplin, OsmAnd, Retro Music, VLC, Tasks, Audio Recorder and OpenTracks. Package-family matching includes variants such as OsmAnd Plus and VLC beta. Inspected AMEX episodes and ScreenQA Rico package joins have **zero hits** for those families. No unambiguous target-family app-name hit was found in inspected goal/history text or Odyssey index names. That is weaker evidence for AC/Odyssey because their published rows have no package column. An expanded alias scan also finds five AC rows from episodes `12689` and `19749` saying **Calendar Pro**. The [screenshots](data_v2_cache/visual/ac_calendar_sheet.jpg) confirm that display name, including a splash screen, but do not establish the package or prove it is Simple Calendar Pro. Generic Voice Recorder aliases also require package resolution. These remain unresolved, alongside Odyssey Calendar; do not treat absence of a regex match as an exclusion certificate. [Additional alias scan](data_v2_cache/additional_name_scan.json), [AC row manifest](data_v2_cache/ac_calendar_samples.json).

Under the literal question “any AndroidWorld app left?”, **yes**: `train/screenqa/rico2468/2455` maps to **`com.android.chrome`**; AMEX episode `c8b2a7b567c442908052d2e34925d084` includes that exact package; AC `12818/0` opens **Settings**, `10651/0` opens **Clock**, and `6981/0` opens **Contacts**. Odyssey random sample 33 is a Settings screenshot. These shared built-in apps are outside the builder's 14-family list. Treat them as an explicitly documented policy choice, not proof of leaked AW-generated tasks. [Package evidence](data_v2_cache/extra_checks.json).

**Visual review.** Samples were drawn without replacement from each new source's downloaded train rows, sorted by published row ID, with seeds `20261117`, `20261118`, `20261119` for Odyssey, AMEX and ScreenQA respectively. The [complete 120-row ledger](data_v2_cache/visual_review.md) links each judgment to its exact image, row ID and action; [machine-readable ledger](data_v2_cache/visual_review.json) and [selection manifest](data_v2_cache/visual_samples.json) preserve the sampling evidence.

| New source | Random rows reviewed | Action coverage | Plausible | Reject | Uncertain |
|---|---:|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Odyssey | 40 | 26 click, 6 home, 5 type, 2 scroll, 1 infeasible | 38 | 2 | 0 |
| AMEX | 40 | 26 click, 6 scroll, 6 status, 2 Enter | 34 | 3 | 3 |
| ScreenQA | 40 | 40 answer | 38 | 1 | 1 |

“Plausible” means the visible label is defensible at the published image size, not that the action was replayed successfully or the entire goal was verified. **51/52 random point labels** plausibly hit a relevant visible control; one of those hits nevertheless selects the wrong email recipient. ScreenQA's uncertain example, `rico36416/35110`, expands unlabeled icons into the full name “Fake GPS - Search location,” which cannot be read from the screenshot. Do not report these small samples as precise whole-source error rates.

Additional checks covered eight AMEX text rows, eight Odyssey complete rows, the rotated-metadata point, and ten sides of the five leak pairs. The text supplement found six plausible inputs, the “mediation” typo, and `esc` on an article. Of the eight Odyssey completion examples, three have plausible end-state evidence, **two remain Pending**, and three remain uncertain: an empty TickTick view behind a subscription dialog, a blank Triller screen, and a loading YouTube page. Their histories are real earlier source actions, but that does not prove success. [Text sheet](data_v2_cache/visual/types_sheet_1.jpg), [terminal sheet](data_v2_cache/visual/terminals_sheet_1.jpg), [targeted manifest](data_v2_cache/supplement_samples.json).

**Mix measured from the three train shards.** Counts below are recomputed from published row labels, not copied from `stats.json`.

| Action | AndroidControl | Odyssey | AMEX | ScreenQA |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| click | 5,751 | 1,264 | 801 | 0 |
| long_press | 26 | 14 | 0 | 0 |
| scroll | 1,243 | 172 | 264 | 0 |
| input_text | 729 | 213 | 182 | 0 |
| keyboard_enter | 0 | 0 | 89 | 0 |
| navigate_back | 365 | 8 | 8 | 0 |
| navigate_home | 2 | 153 | 0 | 0 |
| open_app | 616 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| wait | 637 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| status complete | 1,124 | 93 | 99 | 0 |
| status infeasible | 0 | 24 | 26 | 0 |
| answer | 0 | 0 | 0 | 1,097 |
| **Total** | **10,493** | **1,941** | **1,469** | **1,097** |
| Status share within source | 10.71% | 6.03% | 8.51% | 0% |

Source shares are **69.95% AC / 12.94% Odyssey / 9.79% AMEX / 7.31% ScreenQA**. Overall status share is **1,366/15,000 = 9.11%**; answer share is **7.31%**. Since the AW harness also terminates after `answer`, **16.42%** of sampled labels end an episode under that harness. v1's same-revision cached metadata contains 99,216 rows, **13,573 status rows (13.68%)**, and no answer or keyboard_enter targets. These numbers measure label allocation, not terminal correctness.

The three fully inspected dev splits contain exactly **1,000/500/499 rows**, matching the build statistics. Their action counts also match after combining complete/infeasible into status. Local and pushed stats are identical; all five logs contain their expected summaries and no traceback. Those agreements establish consistency of reporting, not correctness of labels or the unseen train shards. [Log audit](data_v2_cache/log_audit.json), [counts](data_v2_cache/checks.json).

**Quotas and caps.** The quota-as-ceiling rule works for the current disjoint predicates: filler skips every quota category. Reconstructing AMEX's entire small annotation archive yields **34,450 train candidates** and, before image validation, 8,000 selected rows: 500 Enter, 500 complete, 150 infeasible, 1,000 type, 4,424 click, 1,406 scroll, 18 back, 2 home. This matches the builder's claimed 7,998 materialized rows after its reported removal of one click and one infeasible row; those two image removals were not independently checked against raw media. Every inspected train AMEX key belongs to the reconstructed selection. The cap defect is in the quota passes, not the filler; fixing it can preserve the allocation without adding duplicate rows. ScreenQA's observed train per-screen cap and Odyssey's observed train per-episode cap hold in this sample. AC has no episode cap and uses uniform row sampling rather than the plan's app/goal/action balancing.

**Implications for the 2B model.** The promising additions are real screen answers, diverse apps, and explicit actions absent from v1. The most concrete regression risks are premature stopping, silent responses to information requests, typing special-key names, learning scrolls the executor cannot perform, and double submission after typing. Generic AMEX thoughts also weaken the useful target/state descriptions that v1 puts into history; blanking them avoids supervising meaningless prose but does not magically supply missing state. Odyssey histories average **5.30 entries / 688 user-text characters**, versus AC **3.29 / 436**; verify cross-app memory sufficiency and actual token throughput rather than assuming a row always costs the same GPU time. No accuracy improvement or regression was measured here. After repair, use the plan's equal-budget AC-only control on clean development splits, and keep frozen AC TEST/AW outcomes out of mix tuning.

**Evidence and reproduction.** All new downloads, source annotations, range-read v1 metadata, dependency cache, snapshots and images are under the already gitignored `results/codex/data_v2_cache/`. The user's HF token was read for authentication; it was not copied into artifacts, and `HF_HOME` was set inside this directory before HF library use. No builder, trainer or executor edits were made by this audit.

The independent source joins use the [official AC split](https://storage.googleapis.com/gresearch/android_control/splits.json), [Odyssey revision 61632d0f](https://huggingface.co/datasets/hflqf88888/GUIOdyssey/tree/61632d0f), [AMEX revision 17196b29](https://huggingface.co/datasets/Yuxiang007/AMEX/tree/17196b29), [Google ScreenQA annotations](https://github.com/google-research-datasets/screen_qa), and the [original Rico package map](https://storage.googleapis.com/crowdstf-rico-uiuc-4540/rico_dataset_v0.1/ui_details.csv). Downloaded hashes/URLs are in [source manifest](data_v2_cache/sources/manifest.json); dataset hashes are in [download manifest](data_v2_cache/download_manifest.json); final local code/plan snapshots and hashes are in [input manifest](data_v2_cache/input_manifest.json).

Audit code: [download](data_v2_download.py), [source fetch](data_v2_source_fetch.py), [published-row checks](data_v2_audit.py), [raw provenance and sampler reconstruction](data_v2_provenance.py), [random visual selection/overlays](data_v2_visuals.py), [targeted images](data_v2_supplement.py), [visual judgment ledger](data_v2_review_ledger.py), [actual executor probe](data_v2_executor_probe.py). The ledger records manual visual judgments; rerunning it does not substitute for inspecting images.

With the populated cache, rerun the independent checks in this order:

```sh
python3 results/codex/data_v2_audit.py
python3 results/codex/data_v2_visuals.py
python3 results/codex/data_v2_provenance.py
python3 results/codex/data_v2_supplement.py
python3 results/codex/data_v2_review_ledger.py
python3 results/codex/data_v2_executor_probe.py
```

**Final decision: NO-GO.** Rebuild with a screen-disjoint dev split; resolve the type/Enter executor contract; quarantine unsupported gestures/key-like text and invalid trajectory suffixes; review terminal and answer semantics; resolve held-out app provenance; enforce caps in quota selection. Then run these assertions over **every** output row on the builder machine and repeat the visual checks before spending GPU hours. The corrected loader and passing schema/history checks are useful, but they do not offset the demonstrated data failures.

